        },
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "FAQPage",
      mainEntity: [
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "What is the best protein powder for a sensitive stomach?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "An unflavored whey isolate such as Klean Isolate or Isopure Zero Carb is the best starting point if you tolerate dairy, because it is low in lactose and has almost nothing added. If dairy is the issue, a single-ingredient pea or egg white powder such as Naked Pea or Naked Egg is the better choice. Start with half a scoop in water."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Does protein powder cause constipation?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Not usually on its own, but it can contribute. Shakes that replace fiber-rich meals, low fluid intake, dairy and added minerals such as calcium or iron can all play a part. Keep whole foods as the base of your diet, aim for the fiber recommended for your age and sex, and drink more water on high-protein days. See a doctor if it persists."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Does protein powder make you poop?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "It can. Lactose from whey concentrate or milk, sugar alcohols and fermentable fibers such as inulin can all cause loose stools or urgency. A large shake on an empty stomach can also trigger the normal gastrocolic reflex. Switching to an unflavored isolate mixed with water and sipped with a meal is the quickest way to find the cause."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Why does protein powder make me bloated?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Bloating usually comes from fermentation in the colon. Lactose, sugar alcohols, inulin and chicory root fiber are the most common causes, and stacked gums bother some people. Drinking a large shake quickly adds to it. A whey isolate or single-ingredient powder with no added fiber or sugar alcohols removes most of those triggers."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Is whey isolate easier to digest than whey concentrate?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "For most people with lactose intolerance, yes. Isolate is filtered further and usually carries well under a gram of lactose per scoop, compared with a few grams in many concentrates. Isolate also tends to have fewer added ingredients. People with a milk allergy should avoid both, because both contain milk proteins."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Is there a low-FODMAP protein powder?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Whey isolate, egg white and well-made pea isolate are generally low in FODMAPs as proteins. The FODMAP load usually comes from added inulin, sugar alcohols, fruit powders or whey concentrate. The Monash University FODMAP app lists tested products and serving sizes. If you have IBS, work with a dietitian trained in the low-FODMAP approach rather than guessing."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Should I see a doctor about protein powder stomach problems?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Yes, if symptoms continue after switching to a clean powder or stopping it, or if you have blood in your stool, weight loss you did not intend, symptoms that wake you at night, or severe pain. Anyone with IBS, inflammatory bowel disease, kidney disease or pregnancy should talk to a doctor or registered dietitian before adding a protein supplement."
          }
        }
      ],
    },
